# Manuscript PDF page 159

                                PRESSURE



    The hallway went very quiet. I heard the water fountain. I heard my
own pulse. And in the pause that Vale let open, I felt it come down, the
pressed place, the thumb, and I stood in it, and counted, and got to
eight.
    “We aren’t pursuing the matter,” said Vale, gently. “Everyone has a
bad night. I want you to know that. Neighbors forgive.” He tucked the
portfolio under his arm. “The window is under our policy. It’s only that
it’s recorded. In the event that any question were to arise regarding a
participant’s pattern.”
    “You knew,” I said. My voice came out small. “You knew I’d do it.”
    “Sixty-one percent,” said Vale. “It isn’t a prophecy, Juan Carlos. It’s a
kindness. If you know what someone will do, you can make a place for
it. People spend themselves every day, on every block. We only see that
it isn’t wasted.” He smiled. “Come to the Armory. Bring what you like.
I’ll see you there.”
    He walked away down the corridor. His shoes made no sound on
the linoleum.
    I stood with the page in my hand.
    Every refusal, every rage, every shred of integrity had been a line in
his notebook before I made it. The one thing I’d been proud of, my bad-
tempered refusal to be what they wanted, had been priced.
    Mamá found me by the trophy case. She’d folded the chair under
her arm and had her purse in the other hand. She read the page over
my shoulder. Her mouth thinned.
    “¿Qué dice?”
    “It says I do what I do.”
    She took the page out of my hand, folded it in four, and put it in her
purse.
    “Then do something else,” said my mother.
    It stayed with me all the way home. On the 4, in the dark, with the
El shaking the car. A man who has a page on you can’t be beaten by be‐
ing unpredictable, because unpredictable is a column.
    So I wouldn’t be the one deciding. Between two stops I opened my
phone and started a list of people who could tell me no. It got long fast.




